1
00:00:09,560 --> 00:00:11,480
*pattering rain*

2
00:00:17,920 --> 00:00:20,240
*mysterious piano music*

3
00:00:22,160 --> 00:00:24,360
(Albrecht)
For one square meter, you need

4
00:00:24,520 --> 00:00:27,120
about 20 milliliters
of carbon disulfide solution.

5
00:00:27,280 --> 00:00:29,400
You can order it
at any pharmacy.

6
00:00:29,560 --> 00:00:32,520
Impossible to trace
who bought what, or where.

7
00:00:34,200 --> 00:00:35,640
And then, this.

8
00:00:39,720 --> 00:00:41,880
One centimeter is enough.

9
00:00:44,560 --> 00:00:48,400
Looks like amber,
but it's white phosphorus.

10
00:00:50,240 --> 00:00:53,160
Warmed up a bit,
and it dissolves right into it.

11
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:55,520
Then poured in here.

12
00:00:55,600 --> 00:00:58,840
Light bulb glass,
it shatters easiest.

13
00:00:59,240 --> 00:01:01,440
Besides, these things
fit nicely in the hand.

14
00:01:04,680 --> 00:01:05,840
At first, nothing happens.

15
00:01:05,920 --> 00:01:08,760
But once the carbon disulfide
evaporates,

16
00:01:08,920 --> 00:01:12,960
the white phosphorus ignites
upon contact with the air.

17
00:01:14,480 --> 00:01:16,560
Could you demonstrate that?

18
00:01:17,040 --> 00:01:18,040
Of course.

19
00:01:20,880 --> 00:01:22,800
When you use it indoors,

20
00:01:22,880 --> 00:01:25,440
all you need
is a steady draft.

21
00:01:26,640 --> 00:01:28,880
Opening a door
is usually enough.

22
00:01:31,160 --> 00:01:35,360
For Göring's operation,
we'll need large quantities of this...

23
00:01:35,960 --> 00:01:37,200
tincture.

24
00:01:38,320 --> 00:01:41,320
As you can see, we've prepared.

25
00:01:45,360 --> 00:01:47,760
And when does the spectacle begin?

26
00:01:47,920 --> 00:01:49,120
Just you wait.

27
00:02:00,120 --> 00:02:01,680
*eerie music*

28
00:02:25,720 --> 00:02:27,000
*rhythmic music*

29
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:53,960
*flapping wings, cooing*

30
00:03:10,480 --> 00:03:12,400
*somber string music*

31
00:03:16,480 --> 00:03:18,680
*music swells*

32
00:03:32,760 --> 00:03:35,560
An assassination attempt
on the Chancellor?

33
00:03:38,800 --> 00:03:40,240
You can't...

34
00:03:40,400 --> 00:03:43,840
We can't just murder
a politician!

35
00:03:44,440 --> 00:03:46,000
A human being!

36
00:03:46,080 --> 00:03:47,080
Yes, we can.

37
00:03:48,080 --> 00:03:50,520
Probably many people.

38
00:03:53,360 --> 00:03:54,760
That's murder.

39
00:03:57,840 --> 00:03:59,200
I won't take part in this.

40
00:03:59,360 --> 00:04:00,720
You don't have to.

41
00:04:01,160 --> 00:04:03,200
I'm not a murderer. / I know.

42
00:04:03,800 --> 00:04:06,520
I... / But you'll become a murderer

43
00:04:06,600 --> 00:04:07,720
if you do this.

44
00:04:08,960 --> 00:04:10,680
*he breathes shakily*

45
00:04:12,040 --> 00:04:14,280
That's why I didn't tell you.

46
00:04:20,519 --> 00:04:22,040
Charlotte. / Don't you dare!

47
00:04:29,480 --> 00:04:30,800
Farewell.

48
00:04:31,480 --> 00:04:33,640
*music grows more dramatic*

49
00:05:03,680 --> 00:05:06,000
*melancholy brass music*

50
00:05:09,760 --> 00:05:12,920
*dramatic string music begins*

51
00:05:19,760 --> 00:05:22,120
*string music swells slowly*

52
00:05:43,840 --> 00:05:45,720
*ominously trembling music*

53
00:06:16,120 --> 00:06:18,400
*music continues*

54
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:51,320
*music continues*

55
00:07:05,800 --> 00:07:07,960
*music becomes more ominous*

56
00:07:25,120 --> 00:07:27,240
*high-pitched, sustained tone*

57
00:07:28,880 --> 00:07:31,440
*tone swells*

58
00:07:41,920 --> 00:07:43,400
*tone cuts off*

59
00:07:49,880 --> 00:07:51,880
*music fades*

60
00:07:56,960 --> 00:07:58,640
They haven't published anything.

61
00:07:58,800 --> 00:08:03,160
Not Le Monde, not Paris-soir,
none of the French newspapers.

62
00:08:03,240 --> 00:08:04,240
Why?

63
00:08:08,480 --> 00:08:10,760
*dark whooshing*

64
00:08:13,720 --> 00:08:15,800
We will draw
our conclusions from that.

65
00:08:18,520 --> 00:08:19,880
Tonight.

66
00:08:20,520 --> 00:08:21,520
Where?

67
00:08:22,120 --> 00:08:25,360
The Nyssen family estate.
Schloss Liebenberg.

68
00:08:25,960 --> 00:08:27,360
Tonight, the most important leaders

69
00:08:27,440 --> 00:08:30,160
of industry and high finance
are meeting there,

70
00:08:30,240 --> 00:08:33,440
the highest-ranking National Socialists,
the Reich Chancellor.

71
00:08:33,880 --> 00:08:37,520
An opportunity like this
won't come a second time.

72
00:08:38,039 --> 00:08:39,039
Edgar.

73
00:08:40,080 --> 00:08:42,760
It is what is called
an unofficial dinner,

74
00:08:42,840 --> 00:08:45,320
taking place tonight at 7:30 PM
in the grand ballroom.

75
00:08:45,480 --> 00:08:46,480
*somber music*

76
00:08:46,560 --> 00:08:49,360
(Edgar) The hosts are
Alfred and Helga Nyssen,

77
00:08:49,440 --> 00:08:52,040
who, together with 12
industrial magnates and bankers,

78
00:08:52,120 --> 00:08:54,880
are receiving a portion
of the NSDAP leadership.

79
00:08:54,960 --> 00:08:57,160
The Reich Chancellor
is also announced.

80
00:08:57,240 --> 00:08:58,960
For our operation to succeed,

81
00:08:59,120 --> 00:09:01,560
it is vital that the number
of persons

82
00:09:01,720 --> 00:09:05,080
who know every detail of the plan
remains small.

83
00:09:05,160 --> 00:09:08,800
In short, we are all
gathered here in this room.

84
00:09:08,960 --> 00:09:13,280
Herr Wegener has hired
a catering firm run by me.

85
00:09:13,360 --> 00:09:17,480
I selected the staff.
They follow only my orders.

86
00:09:18,200 --> 00:09:21,400
The two of us will be on site
and supervise preparations.

87
00:09:21,840 --> 00:09:23,880
You are our outpost.

88
00:09:24,560 --> 00:09:27,920
According to protocol,
Hitler is scheduled to arrive last.

89
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:29,360
At 8:00 PM at the latest.

90
00:09:30,400 --> 00:09:31,800
His seat in the hall

91
00:09:31,960 --> 00:09:35,360
is directly across
from the host, Alfred Nyssen.

92
00:09:36,880 --> 00:09:39,840
Between the two, there is
a recess in the parquet floor.

93
00:09:39,920 --> 00:09:42,080
That is our cache for the bomb.

94
00:09:42,720 --> 00:09:44,360
Shortly before the guests arrive,

95
00:09:44,520 --> 00:09:46,400
I will make sure
the hall is cleared.

96
00:09:46,560 --> 00:09:50,680
Then we must place the dynamite
very quickly and precisely.

97
00:10:00,080 --> 00:10:02,320
There are
two ways to detonate it.

98
00:10:02,400 --> 00:10:05,880
A timer placed directly
on the explosive.

99
00:10:06,040 --> 00:10:08,680
When time runs out,
this device triggers the explosion.

100
00:10:08,840 --> 00:10:11,160
The advantage: we can
get ourselves to safety.

101
00:10:11,240 --> 00:10:12,640
(Wegener) And the disadvantage?

102
00:10:12,720 --> 00:10:15,200
We commit ourselves
to an exact moment.

103
00:10:16,320 --> 00:10:20,320
A wired detonator is
much harder to install.

104
00:10:20,480 --> 00:10:24,080
The cable has to run through a hole
in the floor into the basement,

105
00:10:24,160 --> 00:10:26,920
and from there through a window
to the outside.

106
00:10:28,120 --> 00:10:30,920
You then pull the cable
to your position in the park,

107
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:34,000
connect it to the detonator there,
and at the agreed time,

108
00:10:34,520 --> 00:10:35,560
you fire it.

109
00:10:36,120 --> 00:10:38,160
Target time is 9:00 PM.

110
00:10:38,680 --> 00:10:39,800
So be it.

111
00:10:40,600 --> 00:10:44,520
After the successful assassination,
you fire a signal flare,

112
00:10:44,600 --> 00:10:47,200
so the Doctor,
who is at the Reichstag,

113
00:10:47,360 --> 00:10:50,520
receives the signal
to enter the plenary hall.

114
00:10:54,040 --> 00:10:56,680
In the plenary hall,
there is a microphone

115
00:10:56,760 --> 00:10:59,960
connected around the clock
to the Broadcasting House.

116
00:11:00,040 --> 00:11:02,520
From there, his speech
can be broadcast without delay

117
00:11:02,680 --> 00:11:05,360
to every radio receiver
in the Reich.

118
00:11:06,400 --> 00:11:09,520
Our contact at the radio station
will also wait for the flare signal,

119
00:11:11,120 --> 00:11:14,200
and switch on the dedicated line
to the Reichstag after ten minutes.

120
00:11:16,440 --> 00:11:18,040
And all of Germany will listen,

121
00:11:18,120 --> 00:11:21,000
when you announce the death
of Hitler and his henchmen.

122
00:11:25,760 --> 00:11:27,520
(Helga) It's only natural, Alfred.

123
00:11:27,600 --> 00:11:29,520
The host takes one side,

124
00:11:29,600 --> 00:11:31,800
and the guest of honor
sits across from him.

125
00:11:33,040 --> 00:11:35,000
But why aren't you sitting next to me?

126
00:11:35,080 --> 00:11:38,160
I have another spot,
right close to our guests.

127
00:11:38,240 --> 00:11:40,360
You must be at my side.

128
00:11:40,440 --> 00:11:42,680
The Führer isn't bringing a lady either.

129
00:11:43,360 --> 00:11:47,040
To your left is Göring,
and to your right, von Papen.

130
00:11:47,120 --> 00:11:48,520
*he snorts with a chuckle*

131
00:11:48,600 --> 00:11:50,280
Papen is taking your place.

132
00:11:51,200 --> 00:11:53,520
I'm tending the field
from the proper side,

133
00:11:53,600 --> 00:11:55,840
Alfred, please don't worry.

134
00:11:57,400 --> 00:11:59,320
This humiliation never ends.

135
00:11:59,400 --> 00:12:02,160
(laughing) And your pride
is that of a wimp.

136
00:12:04,520 --> 00:12:06,760
I hold all the cards in my hand,
every single one.

137
00:12:08,080 --> 00:12:11,120
Hitler's predecessor visited me
the other day, Kurt von Schleicher.

138
00:12:11,200 --> 00:12:14,000
He wants me to back him,
and that's exactly what I'll do,

139
00:12:14,160 --> 00:12:16,040
if this Austrian
doesn't toe the line.

140
00:12:16,120 --> 00:12:18,080
Of course, Alfred, I know that.

141
00:12:18,920 --> 00:12:21,600
Where does she want to sit,
my dear wife?

142
00:12:21,680 --> 00:12:23,320
Look, this is where I sit.

143
00:12:23,720 --> 00:12:26,640
(Annemarie) And what do you think?
Who's sitting next to her?

144
00:12:26,720 --> 00:12:28,920
I'll take care
of the Reichstag President,

145
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:30,840
while you deal
with Herr Hitler.

146
00:12:30,920 --> 00:12:32,560
(Annemarie) There you have it, son.

147
00:12:33,480 --> 00:12:35,000
Alfred, aren't you well?

148
00:12:36,800 --> 00:12:38,120
She isn't listening to me.

149
00:12:41,520 --> 00:12:43,120
*doorbell rings*

150
00:12:51,800 --> 00:12:53,440
Well? / Good news.

151
00:12:53,520 --> 00:12:56,040
You're not being sought.
At least not officially.

152
00:12:56,120 --> 00:12:57,120
Are you sure? / Yes.

153
00:12:57,200 --> 00:12:59,480
After court, I went
to police headquarters.

154
00:12:59,560 --> 00:13:02,120
They assured me
there is nothing against you.

155
00:13:02,200 --> 00:13:03,440
Good, very good.

156
00:13:03,520 --> 00:13:06,120
Still, I would recommend... / No.

157
00:13:07,280 --> 00:13:10,320
Take off your coat and come in.
You're needed.

158
00:13:12,360 --> 00:13:13,960
Herr Litten.
Good to have you here.

159
00:13:14,680 --> 00:13:16,000
Good morning, Frau Behnke.

160
00:13:16,080 --> 00:13:18,080
Would you be so kind as to help
Herr Heymann

161
00:13:18,160 --> 00:13:20,240
beat the carpet out in the courtyard?

162
00:13:20,320 --> 00:13:21,800
Then I can mop in here.

163
00:13:22,800 --> 00:13:24,040
*clock ticking*

164
00:13:26,640 --> 00:13:28,480
*Litten and Heymann huffing*

165
00:13:33,320 --> 00:13:34,320
Herr Heymann.

166
00:13:36,400 --> 00:13:38,360
Ah. Thank you.

167
00:13:42,920 --> 00:13:44,480
*door opens*

168
00:13:45,880 --> 00:13:47,200
*door shuts*

169
00:13:50,120 --> 00:13:52,320
You can't go to the editorial office.

170
00:13:53,120 --> 00:13:55,840
Someone has to report on

171
00:13:55,920 --> 00:13:58,360
what's currently happening
in this election campaign.

172
00:13:59,120 --> 00:14:01,400
I will contribute a first-hand report,

173
00:14:01,480 --> 00:14:05,640
straight from inside the new
National Socialist reality.

174
00:14:06,080 --> 00:14:08,120
Maybe that will wake
some people up,

175
00:14:08,200 --> 00:14:11,360
so that in two weeks,
they cast their vote in the right place.

176
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:13,600
Only under public pressure

177
00:14:13,680 --> 00:14:16,160
will they prosecute
Katelbach's murder.

178
00:14:16,240 --> 00:14:18,040
At least as manslaughter.

179
00:14:19,040 --> 00:14:20,440
*water drains*

180
00:14:28,440 --> 00:14:30,360
*indistinct dialogue*

181
00:14:31,600 --> 00:14:35,040
And I will convince the judiciary
of the illegality of these camps,

182
00:14:35,120 --> 00:14:36,560
what was it you called them again?

183
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:38,960
Concentration camps.

184
00:14:39,760 --> 00:14:42,480
I will take legal action
against these illegal prisons.

185
00:14:43,040 --> 00:14:45,240
The law is still on our side.

186
00:14:45,400 --> 00:14:47,760
*mysterious music*

187
00:14:49,480 --> 00:14:51,320
*carpet beating*

188
00:14:52,920 --> 00:14:55,240
(Wels) Thälmann
just wants to make a fool of me.

189
00:14:55,320 --> 00:14:57,200
He won't budge an inch toward us.

190
00:14:57,280 --> 00:14:59,880
Otto, we must act!

191
00:15:00,320 --> 00:15:02,560
This is a one-time affair.

192
00:15:02,720 --> 00:15:05,840
Once the elections are over,
we go our separate ways again.

193
00:15:07,320 --> 00:15:08,960
Let's take the chance.

194
00:15:09,480 --> 00:15:11,760
Just one meeting,
Comrade Chairman.

195
00:15:12,280 --> 00:15:15,120
Now, don't get so formal
all of a sudden, young lady.

196
00:15:16,920 --> 00:15:18,120
*he sighs*

197
00:15:18,200 --> 00:15:19,240
All right, then.

198
00:15:19,880 --> 00:15:21,000
If you insist.

199
00:15:22,280 --> 00:15:23,680
It won't fail on my account.

200
00:15:23,760 --> 00:15:26,560
*she sighs with relief* / *door closes*

201
00:15:26,720 --> 00:15:29,880
(Thälmann) When did you say? / As soon as possible.

202
00:15:30,040 --> 00:15:31,400
*tense music*

203
00:15:39,760 --> 00:15:42,440
All right, then tomorrow morning
at ten sharp.

204
00:15:42,920 --> 00:15:44,480
(Albrecht) They're getting ahead of us.

205
00:15:44,640 --> 00:15:46,920
The meeting point is already set.

206
00:15:48,480 --> 00:15:49,480
When and where?

207
00:15:49,640 --> 00:15:51,800
Tomorrow morning at ten
at the Reichstag.

208
00:15:51,960 --> 00:15:54,160
KPD and SPD. Together?

209
00:15:54,320 --> 00:15:55,680
Looks like it.

210
00:15:56,040 --> 00:15:57,040
(Wendt) What?

211
00:15:58,800 --> 00:16:01,200
This meeting
must not take place under any circumstances.

212
00:16:01,360 --> 00:16:03,280
How are we supposed
to prevent it now?

213
00:16:04,440 --> 00:16:07,480
(Wendt) We could arrest Thälmann and Wels
under some pretext.

214
00:16:07,640 --> 00:16:09,200
That's too transparent.

215
00:16:10,200 --> 00:16:12,000
That's how you make martyrs.

216
00:16:13,440 --> 00:16:15,640
If we do it, it must be an end
once and for all

217
00:16:15,800 --> 00:16:17,040
to this red scum.

218
00:16:18,640 --> 00:16:20,560
We're moving up our operation.

219
00:16:21,680 --> 00:16:24,560
I don't know if that's feasible. / It is.

220
00:16:24,720 --> 00:16:26,040
With the help of the SA.

221
00:16:27,880 --> 00:16:30,600
The decrees are drawn up
and ready in my drawer.

222
00:16:31,680 --> 00:16:34,640
Good. / I'll take care of it.

223
00:16:35,640 --> 00:16:37,840
And the lists
of those to be arrested as well.

224
00:16:38,480 --> 00:16:40,640
I'll have them duplicated
and distributed.

225
00:16:42,720 --> 00:16:44,760
*restless clamor of voices*

226
00:16:44,920 --> 00:16:48,320
All right, lucky day, ladies.

227
00:16:48,480 --> 00:16:50,760
I need everyone today
for Political.

228
00:16:51,520 --> 00:16:53,360
Please head upstairs.

229
00:16:54,680 --> 00:16:56,760
Orderly, if you please!

230
00:16:57,360 --> 00:16:58,960
One after the other!

231
00:17:00,520 --> 00:17:02,560
*music continues*

232
00:17:15,119 --> 00:17:18,760
Stay ready. From now on,
we need to know where you are at all times.

233
00:17:29,280 --> 00:17:33,880
And now, hear the beautiful old German
folk song 'In einem kühlen Grunde'.

234
00:17:34,040 --> 00:17:37,120
Performed by Emil Engels
and Robert Faustmann.

235
00:17:37,560 --> 00:17:42,200
Accompanied by Ludwig Nelsen
and Frederik Baumgarten.

236
00:17:42,360 --> 00:17:44,920
*solemn piano music*

237
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:53,160
*birds chirping*

238
00:17:58,680 --> 00:18:05,680
# Down in a cool valley #

239
00:18:05,760 --> 00:18:12,600
# A mill wheel turns around #

240
00:18:13,560 --> 00:18:21,640
# My sweetheart has vanished #

241
00:18:21,720 --> 00:18:28,480
# Who made that place her home #

242
00:18:29,760 --> 00:18:38,200
# My sweetheart has vanished #

243
00:18:38,280 --> 00:18:46,080
# Who made that place her home #

244
00:18:46,160 --> 00:18:55,200
# When I hear the mill wheel turning #

245
00:18:55,280 --> 00:19:02,560
# I know not what I yearn #

246
00:19:04,200 --> 00:19:13,400
# I'd rather die right now #

247
00:19:13,480 --> 00:19:21,920
# Then all would be at peace #

248
00:19:22,680 --> 00:19:31,360
# I'd rather die right now #

249
00:19:31,520 --> 00:19:41,600
# Then all would be at peace #

250
00:19:44,400 --> 00:19:46,520
*ship's horn*

251
00:19:56,200 --> 00:19:58,440
*loud typewriter clacking*

252
00:20:00,160 --> 00:20:02,480
*tense, pulsating music*

253
00:20:14,160 --> 00:20:16,080
*music builds*

254
00:20:31,480 --> 00:20:34,560
(Böhm) What is this? / (typist) Arrest warrants.

255
00:20:34,640 --> 00:20:36,520
(Böhm, softly) Rath. Ritter.

256
00:20:37,160 --> 00:20:38,640
Who are you doing this for?

257
00:20:39,400 --> 00:20:40,600
Political.

258
00:20:53,960 --> 00:20:55,960
Regierungsrat Wendt
is not available.

259
00:20:56,680 --> 00:20:58,560
What kind of operation is this, Sebald?

260
00:20:58,640 --> 00:21:01,440
I have no knowledge of it whatsoever,
Senior Detective.

261
00:21:01,520 --> 00:21:02,920
Oh, cut it out!

262
00:21:03,920 --> 00:21:04,920
Wait!

263
00:21:05,080 --> 00:21:06,720
*music turns darker*

264
00:21:07,360 --> 00:21:09,760
What are the people on these lists
accused of?

265
00:21:10,200 --> 00:21:11,280
High treason.

266
00:21:11,440 --> 00:21:13,760
We have clear evidence that communists

267
00:21:13,840 --> 00:21:16,080
and Social Democrats
are planning a coup.

268
00:21:16,160 --> 00:21:17,880
It is an extensive network

269
00:21:17,960 --> 00:21:20,240
running through all strata
of society.

270
00:21:20,320 --> 00:21:22,760
Deep into our own ranks.

271
00:21:25,320 --> 00:21:26,320
Hm?

272
00:21:26,880 --> 00:21:28,960
*music grows uneasy*

273
00:21:40,520 --> 00:21:42,680
Fine, thank you very much. Goodbye.

274
00:21:43,680 --> 00:21:45,240
*telephone rings*

275
00:21:45,960 --> 00:21:47,920
Herr Gräf. / Herr Böhm.

276
00:21:48,000 --> 00:21:50,400
(clerk)
Homicide Division, good day.

277
00:21:57,680 --> 00:22:01,320
(whispers) I must speak with Fräulein Ritter.
Immediately.

278
00:22:01,400 --> 00:22:03,440
Do you know where I can find her?

279
00:22:05,600 --> 00:22:08,240
You mean because of the lists? / Mhm.

280
00:22:08,960 --> 00:22:10,840
I've been phoning everywhere.

281
00:22:11,000 --> 00:22:13,040
(Gennat)
Senior Detective, Herr Gräf,

282
00:22:13,200 --> 00:22:14,960
please, come along.

283
00:22:16,680 --> 00:22:19,120
*restless music continues louder*

284
00:23:00,520 --> 00:23:02,040
*music fades*

285
00:23:02,680 --> 00:23:04,120
Dr. Dresse?

286
00:23:08,040 --> 00:23:09,920
*man shouts indistinctly*

287
00:23:10,000 --> 00:23:12,760
What can you tell me
about Hitler's medical file?

288
00:23:13,120 --> 00:23:14,400
Not so loud.

289
00:23:24,960 --> 00:23:26,880
Who has the file, and what's in it?

290
00:23:31,040 --> 00:23:32,280
Well?

291
00:23:32,840 --> 00:23:34,160
I don't have the file.

292
00:23:34,240 --> 00:23:36,440
Not surprising.
That would be your death warrant.

293
00:23:38,160 --> 00:23:40,640
For me or for you,
it would be a death warrant.

294
00:23:40,720 --> 00:23:44,200
But for others, that file
is a life insurance policy,

295
00:23:44,360 --> 00:23:45,840
if you know what I mean.

296
00:23:45,920 --> 00:23:47,320
Who are you talking about?

297
00:23:47,960 --> 00:23:49,560
*ship's horn*

298
00:23:51,800 --> 00:23:54,040
*ship's horn drowns out dialogue*

299
00:24:04,120 --> 00:24:06,560
*peculiar, tense music*

300
00:24:08,760 --> 00:24:11,440
*indistinct shouting*

301
00:24:23,680 --> 00:24:25,040
It worked.

302
00:24:31,240 --> 00:24:35,080
They'll let you through with that.
Tonight.

303
00:24:44,520 --> 00:24:46,000
An SA ID?

304
00:24:47,200 --> 00:24:48,600
*he laughs out loud*

305
00:24:55,800 --> 00:24:57,280
*music fades*

306
00:24:58,640 --> 00:25:00,080
What are you doing here?

307
00:25:00,720 --> 00:25:04,080
The Chief Inspector
informed me of your whereabouts.

308
00:25:04,840 --> 00:25:05,840
Gennat?

309
00:25:07,080 --> 00:25:09,720
Come with me,
I have something to tell you.

310
00:25:17,600 --> 00:25:19,280
*pattering rain*

311
00:25:19,360 --> 00:25:22,600
Do not go to your apartment.
Under no circumstances.

312
00:25:23,880 --> 00:25:27,120
Your name is on a list.
Inspector Rath's as well.

313
00:25:27,280 --> 00:25:29,320
Both of your lives
are in grave danger.

314
00:25:29,400 --> 00:25:32,640
You must leave the city,
you must leave the country.

315
00:25:32,720 --> 00:25:34,000
As quickly as possible.

316
00:25:40,760 --> 00:25:43,800
I do have one question, though,
Fräulein Ritter.

317
00:25:46,480 --> 00:25:48,880
What did Rath intend to do
with the cognac?

318
00:25:55,520 --> 00:25:57,280
Rath wants to blow up Hitler.

319
00:25:59,720 --> 00:26:00,760
When?

320
00:26:00,920 --> 00:26:02,920
I don't know,
I haven't been let in on it.

321
00:26:05,840 --> 00:26:09,000
The whole thing has to be called off.
Wendt knows something.

322
00:26:09,520 --> 00:26:11,480
It's going to go wrong.
You must warn Rath.

323
00:26:12,840 --> 00:26:14,080
How do you know about Wendt?

324
00:26:17,560 --> 00:26:21,400
Like it or not, I'm working
for Regierungsrat Wendt now.

325
00:26:23,160 --> 00:26:25,320
He gave me no choice,
Fräulein Ritter.

326
00:26:25,400 --> 00:26:28,600
He wants the file, not for Hitler,
but for himself.

327
00:26:28,680 --> 00:26:30,160
And he needs me for that.

328
00:26:32,720 --> 00:26:34,320
At least that's what he thinks.

329
00:26:41,400 --> 00:26:43,160
I know where the medical file is.

330
00:26:47,680 --> 00:26:48,960
I didn't hear that.

331
00:26:49,120 --> 00:26:50,640
*keys jingle*

332
00:26:53,600 --> 00:26:56,120
What's this? / From colleague Gräf.

333
00:26:56,200 --> 00:26:57,960
The keys to his apartment.

334
00:27:01,880 --> 00:27:03,640
Charlotte, you must find Rath.

335
00:27:04,480 --> 00:27:05,920
Warn him.

336
00:27:06,000 --> 00:27:07,960
And get out of the city,
both of you.

337
00:27:08,120 --> 00:27:10,360
Inspector Rath doesn't want
any help.

338
00:27:10,880 --> 00:27:12,280
But he has no choice.

339
00:27:22,160 --> 00:27:23,720
(Heymann) Dear colleagues,

340
00:27:24,200 --> 00:27:26,960
because my presence
in our newsroom

341
00:27:27,040 --> 00:27:30,120
could become rather uncomfortable,

342
00:27:30,200 --> 00:27:32,800
I've asked you here today

343
00:27:32,880 --> 00:27:35,880
to discuss the future course
of our paper.

344
00:27:37,520 --> 00:27:39,960
Elections are on March 5th.

345
00:27:40,040 --> 00:27:43,800
Hundreds of opposition members are in custody,
thousands more will follow.

346
00:27:44,240 --> 00:27:45,960
The situation forces us

347
00:27:46,040 --> 00:27:48,840
to abandon our journalistic restraint.

348
00:27:48,920 --> 00:27:52,400
We must now become
campaigners ourselves,

349
00:27:52,480 --> 00:27:53,720
or else we will perish.

350
00:27:56,840 --> 00:27:58,600
Down to daily business.

351
00:28:01,800 --> 00:28:04,000
This obituary for our...

352
00:28:06,400 --> 00:28:08,280
colleague Samuel Katelbach

353
00:28:08,440 --> 00:28:10,400
will be today's front-page story.

354
00:28:11,360 --> 00:28:13,680
In it, we still name names.

355
00:28:14,480 --> 00:28:17,160
Starting today, on page two,

356
00:28:17,240 --> 00:28:20,560
we will print the names of the disappeared
and the murdered.

357
00:28:20,720 --> 00:28:22,720
We will keep updating this list,

358
00:28:22,800 --> 00:28:24,520
in every edition until the election.

359
00:28:24,600 --> 00:28:28,080
It is likely
that this will have consequences.

360
00:28:28,160 --> 00:28:29,800
I have experienced it
firsthand.

361
00:28:29,880 --> 00:28:32,160
I wouldn't wish the same
on any of you.

362
00:28:33,160 --> 00:28:35,240
You are, of course, free to leave.

363
00:28:35,320 --> 00:28:36,920
I cannot blame you,

364
00:28:37,080 --> 00:28:40,760
after all, I hired reporters,
not martyrs.

365
00:28:40,840 --> 00:28:42,680
The decision is yours.

366
00:28:42,760 --> 00:28:44,840
Only, you must make it soon.

367
00:28:45,320 --> 00:28:46,320
No.

368
00:28:48,040 --> 00:28:49,080
Right now.

369
00:28:52,960 --> 00:28:54,240
*Heymann clears his throat*

370
00:28:55,560 --> 00:28:57,120
I have a family.

371
00:28:58,000 --> 00:28:59,240
I'm sorry.

372
00:29:19,400 --> 00:29:20,880
*door opens*

373
00:29:21,960 --> 00:29:23,040
Good.

374
00:29:24,800 --> 00:29:26,040
To work.

375
00:29:27,680 --> 00:29:30,640
"Dear Herr Heymann,
dear Herr Litten,

376
00:29:31,160 --> 00:29:33,360
now that Herr Katelbach
is no longer with me,

377
00:29:33,440 --> 00:29:34,520
I have decided

378
00:29:34,680 --> 00:29:36,600
to undertake alone a venture

379
00:29:36,680 --> 00:29:38,240
that the two of us had planned.

380
00:29:39,880 --> 00:29:41,400
You will not find me here

381
00:29:41,480 --> 00:29:43,640
when you return
to this apartment.

382
00:29:43,720 --> 00:29:45,240
I therefore take the liberty

383
00:29:45,320 --> 00:29:48,840
of including a few practical
and necessary explanations,

384
00:29:48,920 --> 00:29:52,200
adherence to which should make
your stay on Bayreuther Strasse

385
00:29:52,360 --> 00:29:54,280
as pleasant as possible.

386
00:29:55,200 --> 00:29:56,760
First, the rent.

387
00:29:57,280 --> 00:29:59,840
It is payable in the amount
of 88 Reichsmarks

388
00:29:59,920 --> 00:30:03,160
to Ministerial Director (ret.)
Gustav Roma,

389
00:30:03,240 --> 00:30:07,000
who expects payment promptly
by the fourth of every month.

390
00:30:08,280 --> 00:30:10,000
*gentle piano music*

391
00:30:10,800 --> 00:30:12,920
*typewriter clatters*

392
00:30:22,440 --> 00:30:24,880
A daily two-minute blast of fresh air

393
00:30:24,960 --> 00:30:27,280
prevents mildew from forming
in the rooms,

394
00:30:27,360 --> 00:30:30,360
and otherwise ensures
a pleasant indoor climate.

395
00:30:31,400 --> 00:30:34,640
Should you wish to sublet my room,

396
00:30:34,800 --> 00:30:36,680
it is advisable to divide the costs

397
00:30:36,760 --> 00:30:38,760
according to size.

398
00:30:39,480 --> 00:30:42,640
Quiet and tidy tenants
are to be preferred.

399
00:30:43,520 --> 00:30:44,960
I leave my hometown

400
00:30:45,040 --> 00:30:47,440
with one suitcase
and a modest nest egg,

401
00:30:47,520 --> 00:30:49,400
which, thanks to a certain frugality,

402
00:30:49,480 --> 00:30:51,840
I was even able to grow
over many years.

403
00:30:54,560 --> 00:30:57,240
I will travel for a while
and attempt,

404
00:30:57,400 --> 00:31:00,960
with a watchful eye and keen mind,
to report on it.

405
00:31:04,080 --> 00:31:06,400
To both of you, I wish the very best,

406
00:31:07,320 --> 00:31:10,440
strength and good fortune
in all your endeavors.

407
00:31:12,360 --> 00:31:17,480
Warmest regards to you both,
your friend Elisabeth Behnke."

408
00:31:17,560 --> 00:31:19,560
*somber string music*

409
00:31:21,800 --> 00:31:23,800
*traffic noise*

410
00:31:34,360 --> 00:31:37,160
*announcements, hubbub of voices*

411
00:31:43,360 --> 00:31:45,480
*conductor blows whistle*

412
00:31:46,600 --> 00:31:48,920
*solemn piano music*

413
00:31:51,360 --> 00:31:55,960
(Katelbach) "Elisabeth, one can only write
about Vienna without pathos,

414
00:31:56,040 --> 00:31:58,920
with a smile and a grumble.

415
00:31:59,080 --> 00:32:00,760
Yet in that very manner,

416
00:32:00,840 --> 00:32:03,560
one can be enchanted by a city

417
00:32:03,640 --> 00:32:05,400
and fall in love with it.

418
00:32:05,840 --> 00:32:09,080
And perhaps it isn't
the worst way at all.

419
00:32:09,160 --> 00:32:13,560
Those who truly love
don't sing love songs."

420
00:32:23,560 --> 00:32:27,120
Herr Heymann, one more question.
Do you know where Inspector Rath is?

421
00:32:27,280 --> 00:32:29,160
Rath? No.

422
00:32:29,720 --> 00:32:32,200
I haven't seen him
since our escape.

423
00:32:32,280 --> 00:32:33,280
Thank you.

424
00:32:33,440 --> 00:32:34,840
And not a word about Dresse.

425
00:32:35,000 --> 00:32:37,520
Of course, Frau Ritter.
Keep it strictly confidential.

426
00:32:37,600 --> 00:32:39,120
Thank you. Goodbye.

427
00:32:43,080 --> 00:32:45,960
Potocznik, you won't believe this.

428
00:32:46,120 --> 00:32:48,440
I'll bet anything
you can't guess

429
00:32:48,520 --> 00:32:51,040
who requested the medical file
most recently.

430
00:32:51,120 --> 00:32:52,120
Who?

431
00:32:53,040 --> 00:32:55,080
Ten to one
you're wrong.

432
00:32:55,520 --> 00:32:56,760
Um...

433
00:32:57,640 --> 00:32:58,640
Hindenburg?

434
00:32:58,720 --> 00:33:01,320
Not bad.
Try again, one floor down.

435
00:33:02,120 --> 00:33:04,080
*tense, pulsating music*

436
00:33:07,240 --> 00:33:11,160
(Heymann) Herr Reichskanzler,
forgive the late disturbance.

437
00:33:11,520 --> 00:33:12,680
(Schleicher) What is it?

438
00:33:15,200 --> 00:33:17,640
Out with it. I have
no secrets from my wife.

439
00:33:22,800 --> 00:33:25,800
I am looking for
Adolf Hitler's medical file.

440
00:33:25,960 --> 00:33:27,160
It was drawn up in 1918

441
00:33:27,240 --> 00:33:30,240
during his stay
at the Pasewalk military hospital.

442
00:33:30,320 --> 00:33:33,880
And I know from a reliable source,
that is, first-hand,

443
00:33:34,040 --> 00:33:36,240
that it is in your possession.

444
00:33:39,480 --> 00:33:40,920
What do you want with that document?

445
00:33:41,080 --> 00:33:42,520
To publish it.

446
00:33:43,800 --> 00:33:47,080
If it says what I think it says,

447
00:33:47,160 --> 00:33:48,840
the public needs to know.

448
00:33:52,880 --> 00:33:55,080
It belongs in public view, Kurt.

449
00:33:57,640 --> 00:33:58,760
Yes.

450
00:33:59,840 --> 00:34:01,200
Yet I say no.

451
00:34:01,920 --> 00:34:03,240
Why?

452
00:34:03,320 --> 00:34:05,880
This medical evaluation
of my successor,

453
00:34:05,960 --> 00:34:08,159
I will use it to protect the Reich,

454
00:34:08,239 --> 00:34:10,719
should that gentleman ever dare
to harm our country.

455
00:34:10,800 --> 00:34:12,159
He's already doing that.

456
00:34:12,320 --> 00:34:14,520
In a very substantial way,
I would say.

457
00:34:14,600 --> 00:34:17,239
I myself was interned
in one of those illegal camps.

458
00:34:17,320 --> 00:34:20,400
One of my editors sacrificed his life
in our escape.

459
00:34:20,560 --> 00:34:24,239
Thousands of innocent people
are being arrested across Germany right now.

460
00:34:25,719 --> 00:34:26,880
He's right, Kurt.

461
00:34:27,360 --> 00:34:29,760
You must play this card
before the election.

462
00:34:30,400 --> 00:34:31,520
Now.

463
00:34:40,880 --> 00:34:41,880
No.

464
00:34:42,520 --> 00:34:43,560
Not yet.

465
00:34:45,920 --> 00:34:47,360
(Frau Schleicher) Come along.

466
00:34:50,199 --> 00:34:52,360
*ominously pulsing music*

467
00:35:10,160 --> 00:35:12,360
*festive string music*

468
00:35:21,400 --> 00:35:22,680
*loud knocking*

469
00:35:22,760 --> 00:35:23,920
*music abruptly stops*

470
00:35:24,760 --> 00:35:26,040
Brunner, NSDAP.

471
00:35:26,200 --> 00:35:28,240
Security inspection.
Where is the dining hall?

472
00:35:30,240 --> 00:35:31,320
Right this way.

473
00:35:33,560 --> 00:35:34,920
Everyone out.

474
00:35:35,080 --> 00:35:36,880
Out. Security inspection.

475
00:35:40,360 --> 00:35:42,840
*occasional patting down*

476
00:35:51,560 --> 00:35:52,800
You, maître d'!

477
00:35:53,520 --> 00:35:56,040
Come here, I have
something to discuss with you.

478
00:35:57,840 --> 00:36:00,360
For me, observing
the specific etiquette

479
00:36:00,440 --> 00:36:03,200
this evening
is of paramount importance.

480
00:36:03,280 --> 00:36:05,920
Food and drinks are to be served
first to the hosts,

481
00:36:06,000 --> 00:36:07,600
to you and your wife,

482
00:36:07,680 --> 00:36:09,640
only after that
will the guests of honor be served.

483
00:36:09,720 --> 00:36:11,440
Yes, certainly, but...

484
00:36:12,160 --> 00:36:13,240
You know what?

485
00:36:14,760 --> 00:36:16,680
My wife isn't sitting
next to me at all.

486
00:36:16,760 --> 00:36:17,760
Mmm.

487
00:36:18,600 --> 00:36:20,240
Come, I'll show you.

488
00:36:21,360 --> 00:36:23,160
*peculiar music*

489
00:36:24,600 --> 00:36:26,160
She's sitting over here.

490
00:36:27,640 --> 00:36:32,520
Therefore, I am the only one
to receive preferential service.

491
00:36:33,480 --> 00:36:37,160
First me, and then everyone else.

492
00:36:37,240 --> 00:36:39,720
All at the same time.
My wife included.

493
00:36:39,880 --> 00:36:43,640
Wegener speaks very highly
of your catering expertise.

494
00:36:43,720 --> 00:36:45,160
Do not disappoint me.

495
00:36:50,000 --> 00:36:51,080
What's that?

496
00:36:55,000 --> 00:36:56,760
Those are ventilation grates.

497
00:36:57,480 --> 00:36:58,560
Check them.

498
00:37:04,960 --> 00:37:06,400
*tense music*

499
00:37:12,560 --> 00:37:13,640
Clean.

500
00:37:14,240 --> 00:37:17,080
Everything inspected? / Yes, Herr Obersturmbannführer.

501
00:37:17,160 --> 00:37:18,280
Fall out!

502
00:37:34,160 --> 00:37:36,720
*music grows more dramatic*

503
00:37:42,360 --> 00:37:43,720
Come along.

504
00:38:10,600 --> 00:38:11,880
*faint rumbling*

505
00:38:28,800 --> 00:38:29,800
(Wegener) Rath?

506
00:38:53,080 --> 00:38:56,560
(Helga) Please let me in.
Please let me in!

507
00:38:57,360 --> 00:38:58,360
Alfred!

508
00:39:00,480 --> 00:39:02,440
What on earth is going on here?

509
00:39:03,680 --> 00:39:04,920
Frau Nyssen.

510
00:39:05,000 --> 00:39:08,240
Wegener, why are all the people
standing around outside?

511
00:39:09,440 --> 00:39:12,080
(Edgar) Security inspection,
Frau Nyssen.

512
00:39:13,000 --> 00:39:14,840
They were all just here a moment ago.

513
00:39:15,000 --> 00:39:17,040
(sighs) We also have to check
the entire room

514
00:39:17,120 --> 00:39:19,600
for possible explosive caches.

515
00:39:20,360 --> 00:39:22,120
Oh, I see. Of course.

516
00:39:23,920 --> 00:39:25,200
My goodness.

517
00:39:26,320 --> 00:39:27,360
*she hesitates*

518
00:39:27,800 --> 00:39:30,240
In the floor under the table,
the air grates.

519
00:39:30,400 --> 00:39:33,680
Did you see them? / Just checked them, everything in order.

520
00:39:34,320 --> 00:39:35,320
Thank you.

521
00:39:36,000 --> 00:39:37,520
That's what we're here for. / Good.

522
00:39:38,280 --> 00:39:39,280
Where is my husband?

523
00:39:39,440 --> 00:39:41,600
In the private quarters,
ma'am.

524
00:39:41,680 --> 00:39:43,600
*she sighs irritably*

525
00:39:45,600 --> 00:39:48,040
*suspenseful music*

526
00:40:10,040 --> 00:40:12,120
*music builds dramatically*

527
00:40:13,400 --> 00:40:14,480
Well then...

528
00:40:23,960 --> 00:40:25,120
*hissing*

529
00:40:36,200 --> 00:40:38,560
*music intensifies dramatically*

530
00:40:56,560 --> 00:40:58,240
*music fades*

531
00:40:59,080 --> 00:41:00,680
*gentle swing music*

532
00:41:00,760 --> 00:41:03,920
*peals of laughter, applause*

533
00:41:04,080 --> 00:41:05,840
It'll be fine. Ah!

534
00:41:06,440 --> 00:41:07,480
(giggles) Oh.

535
00:41:07,640 --> 00:41:09,720
*cheering*

536
00:41:09,800 --> 00:41:14,560
Now, my dear
and naughty boys and girls,

537
00:41:14,640 --> 00:41:20,200
she's finally back with us
after a long stay abroad.

538
00:41:20,280 --> 00:41:24,880
The one and only,
divine Habanera!

539
00:41:25,760 --> 00:41:27,600
*cheering*

540
00:41:27,760 --> 00:41:31,160
*"It's All the Jews' Fault"
by Friedrich Hollaender*

541
00:41:32,520 --> 00:41:38,000
# Whether it rains, whether it hails,
whether it snows or lightning flashes #

542
00:41:38,080 --> 00:41:43,320
# Whether it's dusk, whether it thunders,
whether you freeze or whether you sweat #

543
00:41:43,480 --> 00:41:49,040
# Whether it's fair, whether it's overcast,
whether it thaws or whether it pours #

544
00:41:49,120 --> 00:41:55,480
# Whether it trickles, whether it drizzles,
whether you cough, whether you sneeze #

545
00:41:57,960 --> 00:42:01,280
# The Jews are to blame for everything #

546
00:42:01,360 --> 00:42:04,000
# The Jews are to blame for it all #

547
00:42:04,160 --> 00:42:06,640
# Why so, why are they to blame? #

548
00:42:06,720 --> 00:42:09,160
# Child, you don't understand,
they're to blame #

549
00:42:09,240 --> 00:42:11,920
# And screw you too!
They're to blame #

550
00:42:12,000 --> 00:42:14,680
# The Jews are
and are and are to blame #

551
00:42:14,840 --> 00:42:17,480
# And if you don't believe it,
they're to blame #

552
00:42:17,560 --> 00:42:22,240
# For everything, everything,
the Jews are to blame #

553
00:42:22,400 --> 00:42:24,120
(all) Oh, I see!

554
00:42:24,280 --> 00:42:25,720
*cheerful laughter*

555
00:42:30,280 --> 00:42:34,760
# Whether the line is busy,
whether the bathtub leaks #

556
00:42:34,840 --> 00:42:37,280
# Whether your taxes
are assessed wrong #

557
00:42:37,360 --> 00:42:39,400
# Whether the sausage tastes like soap #

558
00:42:39,560 --> 00:42:45,200
# Whether on Sunday the baker is shut,
whether the Prince of Wales is gay #

559
00:42:45,360 --> 00:42:51,320
# Whether at night the furniture creaks,
whether your dog has hard stool #

560
00:42:52,680 --> 00:42:54,000
*music cuts off*

561
00:42:54,160 --> 00:42:56,120
*restless murmuring*

562
00:42:57,080 --> 00:42:59,920
What's the matter?
The boys are with me.

563
00:43:01,040 --> 00:43:02,120
Play!

564
00:43:02,480 --> 00:43:04,880
*kiss sound, giggling*

565
00:43:05,040 --> 00:43:08,320
Go on, play! Keep playing, girls!

566
00:43:14,840 --> 00:43:17,120
# That the snow
is so terribly white #

567
00:43:17,280 --> 00:43:19,720
# And what's more, so they say, cold #

568
00:43:19,800 --> 00:43:24,960
# That fire, by contrast, is hot
And that trees stand in the woods #

569
00:43:25,120 --> 00:43:30,160
# That a rose isn't an onion
and that tartare is ground #

570
00:43:30,320 --> 00:43:38,320
# That Heine isn't half bad
and that Einstein is quite gifted #

571
00:43:38,400 --> 00:43:44,760
(Blank, bellowing)
# The Jews are to blame for everything #

572
00:43:44,840 --> 00:43:47,760
*SA men chime in*
# The Jews are to blame for everything #

573
00:43:47,920 --> 00:43:50,840
# The Jews are to blame for everything #

574
00:43:50,920 --> 00:43:53,480
# The Jews are
and are to blame for everything #

575
00:43:53,560 --> 00:43:55,960
# The Jews are to blame for everything #

576
00:43:56,120 --> 00:43:58,520
# The Jews are to blame for everything #

577
00:43:58,680 --> 00:44:01,120
# The Jews are to blame for everything #

578
00:44:01,200 --> 00:44:03,520
# The Jews are
and are to blame for everything #

579
00:44:03,600 --> 00:44:05,760
# The Jews are to blame for everything #

580
00:44:05,920 --> 00:44:08,320
# The Jews are
and are to blame for everything #

581
00:44:08,400 --> 00:44:10,920
# The Jews are to blame for... #

582
00:44:11,080 --> 00:44:12,960
*somber tones drown out singing*

583
00:44:13,120 --> 00:44:15,000
*heartbeat sound begins*

584
00:44:19,440 --> 00:44:22,120
*cheerful background conversation,
laughter*

585
00:44:26,480 --> 00:44:28,280
*hubbub of voices*

586
00:44:28,880 --> 00:44:29,960
Charlotte? / Reinhold.

587
00:44:30,120 --> 00:44:31,320
How are you? / Fine.

588
00:44:31,400 --> 00:44:34,160
I was worried.
Worried doesn't begin to describe it.

589
00:44:34,320 --> 00:44:35,680
Thank you for the key, too.

590
00:44:36,320 --> 00:44:37,800
Have you seen Gereon?

591
00:44:37,880 --> 00:44:39,160
No. / (Jacoby) Rath?

592
00:44:39,800 --> 00:44:40,920
Yes. / I saw him.

593
00:44:41,080 --> 00:44:43,640
He was at my place
in the broadcasting house this afternoon.

594
00:44:43,720 --> 00:44:46,040
This afternoon? And what did he want?

595
00:44:46,200 --> 00:44:49,040
He asked me to return to the studio,
tonight at 9:00 PM.

596
00:44:49,120 --> 00:44:53,040
Exactly ten minutes after the signal,
you switch on the live line.

597
00:44:53,200 --> 00:44:55,480
*sinister music*

598
00:45:05,120 --> 00:45:06,520
*door shuts*

599
00:45:07,240 --> 00:45:09,320
Reichstag, you say? / Yes.

600
00:45:12,200 --> 00:45:13,800
Change your clothes immediately.

601
00:45:13,880 --> 00:45:15,840
Don't forget your badge.
I'll call a cab.

602
00:45:15,920 --> 00:45:16,920
Now? / Yes, right away!

603
00:45:17,080 --> 00:45:18,440
I'll wait outside for you.

604
00:45:19,880 --> 00:45:21,200
*whistle*

605
00:45:22,720 --> 00:45:24,920
*tense music*

606
00:45:50,760 --> 00:45:53,160
Good evening,
Herr Reichstag President.

607
00:45:58,120 --> 00:46:01,240
Maître d', one last minor detail
has occurred to me.

608
00:46:01,320 --> 00:46:02,960
We are changing the seating arrangement.

609
00:46:04,160 --> 00:46:05,240
I...

610
00:46:09,480 --> 00:46:10,920
will sit here.

611
00:46:15,840 --> 00:46:17,080
Him...

612
00:46:21,240 --> 00:46:23,040
we place over here.

613
00:46:24,080 --> 00:46:25,680
In your seat, Mother.

614
00:46:31,000 --> 00:46:33,440
*music grows more energetic*

615
00:46:47,760 --> 00:46:49,440
(whispering) What do we do now?

616
00:46:51,000 --> 00:46:52,000
*

